// Practice quiz: Events and event bubbling
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/events-and-event-bubbling

::o1 passing a handler::What is wrong with button.addEventListener("click", save())? {
~Click is not a valid event type #Not quite. click is valid.
~Handlers must be arrow functions #Not quite. Any function works.
=It calls save immediately and registers its return value #Correct. Pass the function itself, without parentheses.
~An element can only have one listener #Not quite. An element can have many.
}

::o2 target and currentTarget::A listener on a ul runs when a span inside an li is clicked. What is event.currentTarget? {
~The span #Not quite. That is event.target, where the event started.
=The ul #Correct. currentTarget is the element whose listener is running.
~The li #Not quite. The listener is not on the li.
~The document #Not quite. The event has not been handled there.
}

::o2 stopping::What does event.stopPropagation() do? {
~It cancels the default action #Not quite. That is preventDefault.
~It removes the listener #Not quite. That is removeEventListener.
~It prevents the current listener from finishing #Not quite. The current listener keeps running.
=It stops the event from reaching further ancestors #Correct. Listeners higher up will not run.
}

::o3 delegation benefit::Why does a listener on the parent list still work for items added later? {
=Clicks on the new items bubble up to the parent #Correct. The parent listener sees them without any new registration.
~Browsers copy listeners to new children #Not quite. Listeners are not copied.
~append re-registers all listeners #Not quite. append only attaches a node.
~It does not; each item needs its own #Not quite. Delegation avoids exactly that.
}

::o3 preventDefault::A link click handler calls event.preventDefault(). A listener on an ancestor also exists. Does it run? {
~No, because the event was cancelled #Not quite. Cancelling the default action is separate from propagation.
~Only if the link has an id #Not quite. Ids play no part in this.
~Only after the page navigates #Not quite. The navigation was prevented.
=Yes, because preventDefault does not stop bubbling #Correct. Only stopPropagation stops the rise.
}
